::ALG2_U06_L03_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which model type is suggested by data with a constant rate of change? {
= linear#Correct. A constant rate of change is the defining pattern of a linear model.
~ exponential#Exponential models have a constant ratio, not a constant difference.
~ quadratic#Quadratic models usually have changing first differences.
~ logarithmic#Logarithmic data increases or decreases more slowly over time.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A data set doubles about every step. Which model type is most reasonable? {
~ linear#Linear data changes by equal differences, not repeated factors.
= exponential#Correct. Repeated multiplication by about 2 suggests exponential growth.
~ quadratic#Quadratic growth does not usually double by a constant factor each step.
~ constant#A constant model would not change each step.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Actual value is 71 and predicted value is 75. What is the residual? {
~ 4#This subtracts actual from predicted instead of predicted from actual.
~ 146#This adds the actual and predicted values.
= -4#Correct. Residual = actual - predicted = 71 - 75 = -4.
~ 75#This is only the predicted value.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does a residual of 0 mean? {
~ The model has no equation.#A residual of 0 is about one prediction, not whether an equation exists.
~ The actual value is 0.#The residual can be 0 even when the data value is not 0.
~ The predicted value is 0.#The prediction can be nonzero and still match the actual value.
= The prediction matches the actual value.#Correct. Actual minus predicted equals 0 when they are equal.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which prediction is extrapolation? {
= Predicting sales for month 15 from data collected for months 1 through 10.#Correct. Month 15 is outside the observed data range.
~ Predicting sales for month 6 from data collected for months 1 through 10.#Month 6 is inside the data range, so this is interpolation.
~ Finding the residual for month 3.#Residuals measure prediction errors, not prediction location.
~ Reading the actual value for month 4.#Reading data is not making a model prediction.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which prediction is interpolation? {
~ Predicting year 2035 from data ending in 2025.#This is outside the observed range, so it is extrapolation.
= Predicting x=4 from data collected for x=1 through x=8.#Correct. x=4 lies inside the observed data range.
~ Predicting a value with no data pattern.#Interpolation still needs a model or data pattern.
~ Predicting x=20 from data ending at x=10.#This is outside the observed range.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>For y=4x+9, what is the predicted value when x=6? {
~ 24#This uses only 4x and omits the intercept.
~ 19#This adds 4, 6, and 9 incorrectly.
= 33#Correct. y=4(6)+9=33.
~ 54#This multiplies x by the intercept instead of using the model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>For y=2(3)<sup>x</sup>, what is the predicted value when x=3? {
~ 18#This uses 2 times 3 times 3 instead of the exponent.
~ 24#This adds an extra factor incorrectly.
~ 9#This calculates 3 squared, not the full expression.
= 54#Correct. y=2(3)<sup>3</sup>=54.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which residual pattern suggests a linear model may be a poor fit? {
= Residuals form a curved pattern.#Correct. A pattern in residuals suggests the model is missing structure.
~ Residuals are scattered randomly around 0.#Random residuals around 0 support the model fit.
~ Residuals are small and mixed positive and negative.#Small mixed residuals are generally a good sign.
~ Residuals include both decimals and whole numbers.#Number type does not determine model fit.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which data behavior best supports a quadratic model? {
~ Constant first differences.#That supports a linear model.
= A curved pattern with one turning point.#Correct. Quadratic models often show a parabolic shape with a turning point.
~ A constant ratio between y-values.#That supports an exponential model.
~ No visible relationship.#No visible pattern does not support a quadratic model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A model predicts 120 for a context measured in dollars. How should the prediction be reported? {
~ 120 units#The context gives dollars, so units should be specific.
~ 120 percent#The model output is a dollar amount, not a percent.
= about $120#Correct. The prediction should use the correct contextual units.
~ x=120#This labels the input variable, not the predicted output.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Why can extrapolation be risky? {
~ It always uses exact data values.#Extrapolation uses a model outside the observed data range.
~ It never uses a model.#Extrapolation does use a model or pattern.
~ It only predicts inside the data range.#That describes interpolation.
= The pattern may not continue outside the data range.#Correct. The model may stop being valid beyond observed values.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which table suggests a linear model? {
= x: 1,2,3; y: 5,8,11#Correct. The y-values increase by 3 each time.
~ x: 1,2,3; y: 3,9,27#This shows a constant ratio of 3.
~ x: 1,2,3; y: 1,4,9#This follows a quadratic pattern.
~ x: 1,2,3; y: 8,8,8#This is constant rather than a changing linear trend.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which table suggests exponential growth? {
~ x: 1,2,3; y: 4,7,10#This has a constant difference of 3.
= x: 1,2,3; y: 2,6,18#Correct. The y-values multiply by 3.
~ x: 1,2,3; y: 1,4,9#This follows square numbers, so the pattern is quadratic rather than exponential.
~ x: 1,2,3; y: 12,9,6#This is linear decrease.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which model has an initial value of 14 when x=0? {
~ y=14x+2#When x=0, this model gives 2.
~ y=2x+7#When x=0, this model gives 7.
= y=5x+14#Correct. The y-intercept is 14, so the predicted value at x=0 is 14.
~ y=14x#When x=0, this model gives 0.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which model predicts a decreasing exponential relationship? {
~ y=20(1.15)<sup>x</sup>#The factor is greater than 1, so this increases.
~ y=20+1.15x#This is linear, not exponential.
~ y=20(1)<sup>x</sup>#This stays constant because the factor is 1.
= y=20(0.85)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. A factor between 0 and 1 gives exponential decay.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does a positive residual mean? {
= The actual value is greater than the predicted value.#Correct. Actual minus predicted is positive when actual is larger.
~ The predicted value is greater than the actual value.#That would make the residual negative.
~ The model has no error.#A positive residual is still an error unless it equals 0.
~ The x-value is positive.#Residual sign is about prediction error, not the sign of x.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does a negative residual mean? {
~ The actual value is greater than predicted.#That would produce a positive residual.
= The actual value is less than predicted.#Correct. Actual minus predicted is negative when actual is smaller.
~ The model cannot be used at all.#One negative residual does not automatically invalidate the model.
~ The data point has a negative x-value.#Residual sign does not describe the input value.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which is the best reason to include context when interpreting a model? {
~ Context changes arithmetic rules.#The calculations stay the same, but meaning depends on context.
~ Context removes the need for units.#Context makes units more important, not less important.
= Context tells what the variables and predictions represent.#Correct. A model is useful only when its quantities are interpreted.
~ Context guarantees every prediction is accurate.#Context helps judge reasonableness but does not guarantee accuracy.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes a good model choice? {
~ Choose the model with the largest output.#Largest output does not mean best fit.
~ Choose the most complicated model every time.#A complicated model may overfit or ignore context.
~ Choose the model with no variables.#A useful model usually represents relationships with variables.
= Choose a model that fits the pattern and makes sense in context.#Correct. Fit and context both matter.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A scatterplot curves upward faster and faster. Which model is often reasonable? {
= exponential#Correct. Faster and faster growth can indicate an exponential pattern.
~ constant#A constant model would stay flat.
~ linear#Linear growth would increase by equal amounts.
~ absolute value#An absolute value model has a V-shape, not repeated growth.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A predicted temperature is 70, and the actual temperature is 68. What is the residual? {
~ 2#This subtracts actual from predicted.
= -2#Correct. Residual = 68 - 70 = -2.
~ 68#This is the actual value, not the residual.
~ 138#This adds the values instead of finding the error.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which model predicts y=29 when x=4? {
~ y=5x+4#This gives 24 when x=4.
~ y=4x+5#This gives 21 when x=4.
= y=6x+5#Correct. 6(4)+5=29.
~ y=29x#This gives 116 when x=4.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which variable is usually the input in a model y=f(x)? {
~ y#The y-value is usually the output.
~ f(x)#This notation represents the output value.
~ the residual#The residual is prediction error, not the input.
= x#Correct. In y=f(x), x is the input variable.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which question helps evaluate whether a prediction is reasonable? {
= Is the input within the data range and does the output make sense in context?#Correct. Range and context both help judge reasonableness.
~ Is the answer the largest possible number?#Large numbers are not automatically reasonable.
~ Did the model use at least three symbols?#Symbol count does not measure reasonableness.
~ Can the graph be drawn without labels?#Labels help interpretation; ignoring them weakens the model.
}
